1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,454
When you log out,

2
00:00:01,454 --> 00:00:02,857
the server keeps working.

3
00:00:02,857 --> 00:00:04,316
Something has to start it,

4
00:00:04,316 --> 00:00:04,821
watch it,

5
00:00:04,821 --> 00:00:05,663
and restart it.

6
00:00:06,213 --> 00:00:08,643
On Ubuntu that something is system D.

7
00:00:08,643 --> 00:00:09,760
It runs services,

8
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:10,877
keeps their logs,

9
00:00:10,877 --> 00:00:12,847
and starts jobs on a schedule.

10
00:00:13,397 --> 00:00:17,685
Today you read a service file, check it, read its logs, and schedule a job.

11
00:00:19,735 --> 00:00:24,251
A service is described by a unit file.

12
00:00:24,251 --> 00:00:26,984
Read the one for S S H.

13
00:00:26,984 --> 00:00:33,282
Type: head -n 14 /usr/lib/systemd/system/ssh.service.

14
00:00:33,832 --> 00:00:37,161
The Unit section describes the service and when it starts.

15
00:00:37,161 --> 00:00:39,400
The Service section says how to run it.

16
00:00:40,350 --> 00:00:45,086
Exec start is the command that system D runs. The service is that program.

17
00:00:45,636 --> 00:00:48,952
Restart equals on-failure means: if it crashes,

18
00:00:48,952 --> 00:00:50,716
system D starts it again.

19
00:00:50,716 --> 00:00:53,892
That is why services come back by themselves.

20
00:00:55,942 --> 00:01:00,699
Now write a unit file of your own. Here is one. Type: cat web.service.

21
00:01:01,249 --> 00:01:02,620
A small web server.

22
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:06,442
The install section says wanted by multi-user target,

23
00:01:06,442 --> 00:01:08,823
which means start at normal boot.

24
00:01:09,773 --> 00:01:12,103
Check it before you use it.

25
00:01:12,103 --> 00:01:15,643
Type: systemd-analyze verify web.service.

26
00:01:15,643 --> 00:01:22,807
Grep hides one harmless warning about I P version six from this recording computer.

27
00:01:23,357 --> 00:01:25,640
No output means no problems found.

28
00:01:26,790 --> 00:01:30,715
Now a file with mistakes. Type: cat bad.service.

29
00:01:31,265 --> 00:01:37,303
Can you spot them? Check it the same way.

30
00:01:37,853 --> 00:01:38,746
Two problems.

31
00:01:38,746 --> 00:01:40,119
An unknown key name,

32
00:01:40,119 --> 00:01:42,180
because Restart is misspelled.

33
00:01:42,180 --> 00:01:44,515
And a command that does not exist.

34
00:01:44,515 --> 00:01:45,133
Fix both,

35
00:01:45,133 --> 00:01:46,301
and verify again.

36
00:01:48,351 --> 00:01:49,737
Now control a service.

37
00:01:49,737 --> 00:01:52,130
This needs a running system D machine,

38
00:01:52,130 --> 00:01:53,957
such as your virtual machine,

39
00:01:53,957 --> 00:01:56,287
so these screens are an illustration.

40
00:01:57,237 --> 00:02:02,165
Check status first. Type: systemctl status web. Read two lines.

41
00:02:02,715 --> 00:02:06,704
Loaded says the file was found, and enabled means it starts at boot.

42
00:02:07,754 --> 00:02:11,893
Active says running now. If it says failed, read the log lines under it.

43
00:02:12,943 --> 00:02:17,359
Start, stop, restart and reload change the service right now. Use sue do.

44
00:02:17,909 --> 00:02:20,139
Enable and disable control boot.

45
00:02:20,139 --> 00:02:22,160
Enable does not start it now.

46
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:24,181
Add dash dash now to do both.

47
00:02:25,131 --> 00:02:30,976
After you edit a unit file, run: systemctl daemon-reload. Then restart the service.

48
00:02:31,526 --> 00:02:34,620
Then check status again. Always confirm the change.

49
00:02:36,670 --> 00:02:37,943
When a service fails,

50
00:02:37,943 --> 00:02:38,732
read its log.

51
00:02:38,732 --> 00:02:40,854
System D keeps logs in the journal,

52
00:02:40,854 --> 00:02:43,219
and you read them with journal control.

53
00:02:43,769 --> 00:02:49,337
Logs for one service: journalctl -u web. Add dash n to limit the lines.

54
00:02:49,887 --> 00:02:53,599
Read the end first. The last lines usually say why it failed.

55
00:02:54,649 --> 00:02:58,532
Add dash f to follow new lines live. Control C stops it.

56
00:02:59,082 --> 00:03:03,413
Filter by time with dash dash since, and by severity with dash p err.

57
00:03:03,963 --> 00:03:05,989
And dash b shows only the current boot.

58
00:03:08,039 --> 00:03:09,544
How does a server start?

59
00:03:09,544 --> 00:03:10,422
It is a chain.

60
00:03:10,422 --> 00:03:12,429
Firmware starts the boot loader.

61
00:03:12,429 --> 00:03:14,562
The boot loader starts the kernel.

62
00:03:14,562 --> 00:03:16,255
The kernel starts system D,

63
00:03:16,255 --> 00:03:17,447
process number one.

64
00:03:17,997 --> 00:03:20,335
System D then reaches a target,

65
00:03:20,335 --> 00:03:21,843
a group of services.

66
00:03:21,843 --> 00:03:25,464
A server normally reaches multi-user dot target.

67
00:03:26,014 --> 00:03:28,564
To see which services slowed the boot,

68
00:03:28,564 --> 00:03:30,375
run: systemd-analyze blame.

69
00:03:30,375 --> 00:03:31,583
For the boot logs,

70
00:03:31,583 --> 00:03:33,395
use journal control dash b.

71
00:03:35,545 --> 00:03:39,065
System D can also start jobs on a schedule,

72
00:03:39,065 --> 00:03:40,047
with timers.

73
00:03:40,047 --> 00:03:40,538
First,

74
00:03:40,538 --> 00:03:43,567
check a schedule before you trust it.

75
00:03:43,567 --> 00:03:46,105
Type: systemd-analyze calendar.

76
00:03:46,655 --> 00:03:48,255
It shows the normal form,

77
00:03:48,255 --> 00:03:49,919
and when it will next run.

78
00:03:49,919 --> 00:03:52,223
Your date and time zone will differ.

79
00:03:52,223 --> 00:03:52,735
Two a m,

80
00:03:52,735 --> 00:03:53,375
every day.

81
00:03:54,425 --> 00:03:59,012
Weekday mornings. Same command, new schedule.

82
00:03:59,562 --> 00:04:05,087
Monday to Friday, at nine. Always read the next run time before you rely on a schedule.

83
00:04:07,137 --> 00:04:11,724
The older tool is cron. One line has five time fields, then the command.

84
00:04:12,274 --> 00:04:13,139
Minute thirty,

85
00:04:13,139 --> 00:04:13,695
hour two,

86
00:04:13,695 --> 00:04:14,992
any day of the month,

87
00:04:14,992 --> 00:04:15,610
any month,

88
00:04:15,610 --> 00:04:16,660
Monday to Friday.

89
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:18,823
That is two thirty a m on weekdays.

90
00:04:19,873 --> 00:04:24,566
Edit your table with: crontab -e. List it with: crontab -l.

91
00:04:25,116 --> 00:04:30,642
Cron runs with a small environment, so use full paths, and test the command by hand first.

92
00:04:32,692 --> 00:04:34,015
Four myths to clear up.

93
00:04:34,565 --> 00:04:39,706
Myth one: enable starts the service. No. Enable is for boot. Start is for now.

94
00:04:40,256 --> 00:04:45,632
Myth two: edit the unit file and it applies. No. Reload system D, then restart.

95
00:04:46,182 --> 00:04:51,259
Myth three: restart is the first fix. No. Read the log first, or you lose the clue.

96
00:04:51,809 --> 00:04:55,436
Myth four: cron has your normal shell. No. Use full paths.

97
00:04:55,986 --> 00:04:57,650
Read first. Then change.

98
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:02,025
Here is how you check your own understanding.

99
00:05:02,575 --> 00:05:06,010
Read a unit file, and find the command and the restart rule.

100
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:09,696
Check a unit file with system D analyze verify.

101
00:05:10,246 --> 00:05:12,443
Read status, Loaded and Active.

102
00:05:12,993 --> 00:05:15,660
Explain start, enable, and daemon reload.

103
00:05:16,210 --> 00:05:18,535
Read a service log with journal control.

104
00:05:19,085 --> 00:05:22,456
Write a schedule as a cron line, and check a timer schedule.

105
00:05:23,006 --> 00:05:24,627
To pass, you need all six.

106
00:05:26,677 --> 00:05:31,456
Your turn, in your virtual machine. Nothing here installs or changes a service.

107
00:05:32,006 --> 00:05:36,912
One: read the S S H unit file with head, and find exec start and restart.

108
00:05:37,462 --> 00:05:40,972
Two: write your own web dot service in your home folder,

109
00:05:40,972 --> 00:05:41,849
and verify it.

110
00:05:41,849 --> 00:05:43,416
Then break it on purpose,

111
00:05:43,416 --> 00:05:44,481
and verify again.

112
00:05:45,031 --> 00:05:49,682
Three: run system control status S S H, and read Loaded and Active.

113
00:05:50,232 --> 00:05:56,397
Four: read the last ten lines of its log, with journal control dash u S S H dash n ten.

114
00:05:56,947 --> 00:06:00,808
Five: check a timer schedule with system D analyze calendar.

115
00:06:01,358 --> 00:06:06,926
Six: write, on paper, a cron line for two thirty a m on weekdays. Do not install it.

116
00:06:07,476 --> 00:06:10,783
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

117
00:06:26,333 --> 00:06:27,208
Quick check.

118
00:06:27,758 --> 00:06:30,510
Which line in a unit file is the command to run?

119
00:06:31,060 --> 00:06:34,537
Which command makes system D re-read edited unit files?

120
00:06:35,087 --> 00:06:36,858
Which tool reads service logs?

121
00:06:37,408 --> 00:06:39,200
Does enable start a service now?

122
00:06:39,750 --> 00:06:46,235
Objective covered: MC04-O06.

123
00:06:46,785 --> 00:06:48,854
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

124
00:06:59,404 --> 00:07:02,327
Next lesson: installing and updating packages.

125
00:07:02,877 --> 00:07:06,205
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
